ut 10 to 20 percent of those inherent  mrna  the second part this middle column is  that the data itself is incredibly  sparse this ends up being an issue for  some of our computational processing  downstream in terms of efficiency and  weeding out if a zero is an actual zero  or if a zero is a dropout aka the mrna  was there but was lost its at some point  during the experimentation process  and lastly on the right  that there's a high amount of noise and  what this paper what this graph is  showing you is that the counts per gene  of two cells of the exact same cell type  the r squared value is incredibly low  and you'll see things circled on the far  right like these massive number of  dropout events  essentially showing that taking averages  across means of cells is not necessarily  the best practice in interpreting single  cell rna-seq  in order to insert our crispr clean  workflow into the general  10x single cell rna-seq workflow what  you can see on the left is a description  of  our unwanted reads represented in purple  every we remove for the simple crisper  digestion  and as you can see we do this before the  pcr amplification in order to maximize  the number of unique molecules that are  recovered  you can see the workflow on the right  and the timeline incorporated  in total it takes about two hours from  start to finish but the hands-on time is  only 45 minutes  the outline of this presentation is  going to consist of the following  first i'm going to go over our proof of  concept essentially assess the initial  viability of this approach within  cardiovascular samples  second i'm going to talk about a  methodology for designing a robust  technology using a mass amount of  publicly available data and third  i'm going to show you the results of our  application on immune samples  now you guys might be thinking what are  we going to be removing within single  cell rna-seq data is there anything  useless that can be removed  and so to really set the scene what i'm  describing to you here is essentially a  schematic illustrating the reads that go  on the sequencer and the reads that get  used so in this graph on the green on  the bottom arrows the total number of  reads so 100 percent of these reads the  green reads are what goes on the  sequencer  after that about 90 percent or so  confidently aligned to the genome and  are actually used  now what you see on the top is this pink  bar represents that of those that align  confidently to the genome really only  about 50 percent 50 to 60 percent  aligned to the transcriptome and because  this is an rna-seq experiment so when  you do any of your alignment methods the  most canonical being using cell ranger  from the 10x platform  then you're really only taking those  that align to the transcriptome  and so thus you can see this top pink  box  highlighted in this pink dash line  represents that even if you have a  hundred percent of your reads that come  off the sequencer really only about 50  to 60 percent within the sample example  are really being used for downstream  processing  so what i'm showing you here is that  same schematic but overlaid on top  are the regions and the number of reads  that we target  for removal  so you can see here in this light blue  box  are the non-polyadenylated ribosomal rna  that again are totally removed from the  sequencing library are absolutely  ignored those account for about 10 of  the reads and thus we target for them  for removal  in addition you can see in the dark  purple box  about 100 of the top  highly expressed ribosomal and  mitochondrial genes  that are typically removed and do not  play an important part in identifying  unique cell types or cell phenotypes  those are also removed  and together they constitute about 36  percent of your reads  so in order to see if removing those  regions actually has any effect in  redistributing reads to lowly express  transcripts  and unique molecules and thus increasing  the signal to 