eads per  cell  you can see the added value of  performing depletion we recover about a  250 more genes per cell we recover an  additional 2 000 unique molecules per  cell  and because of that what we see is about  an additional 800 or 900 genes that are  incorporated into downstream processing  and thus are biologically relevant  now what i didn't tell you about these  samples is that these are only  endothelial samples so they're really  predominantly only one main cell type  and the goal of this analysis is to  really dissect individual subtypes  within these this one main cell type  so what you can see here  represented in these u-map plots that in  both the standard and depleted there's  only really one cohort of cells with  many different subtypes that cohort  representing the again one main  endothelial type is represented in this  purple dash line  another thing that's fantastic to see is  that the depleted doesn't alter  the underlying transcriptional state of  these cells  represented here that the u maps are  almost a mirror image of each other  now what's very interesting and the  added value of depletion becomes  apparent once we begin looking a little  bit more closely at some of these  subtypes within these endothelial cells  so what i'm doing here is on the next  couple of slides i'm essentially picking  randomly some of these subtypes within  the main cohort of cells  and looking at the differentially  expressed gene relative to all other  subtypes within the sample and that's  being represented here within these  volcano plots  and you can see on the left in the  standard if you're looking at this as a  normal researcher you would look at this  volcano pawn and you would see cohorts  of genes both upregulated and down  regulated pass the log to full change of  two to four  so you would look at this and you would  say this is a massively different  subtype the g the cells represented in  this orange sample are wildly different  from the cells in its surrounding  neighborhood  now if you look on the depleted remember  we've added an additional close to 250  300 genes per cell but more importantly  we've added an additional 2 000 unique  molecules per cell these samples here  represent about 50 000 cells  and so by redistributing those reads to  lowly expressed and essentially reducing  the dropout and noise within the sample  what you can see here is  a greater biological interpretation that  represents the ground truth  the same number of genes are different  amongst the same exact cell type  the only differences is a massive  shrinking and those thresholds in those  log two full change so if you look at  this as a as a researcher now you don't  see those massive differences in genes  that you saw in the standard condition  this again like i said more accurately  represents the ground truth that this is  one main cell type and these are just  different nuanced subtypes within this  main group of endothelial cells  i'm showing you here a different subtype  within the group of endothelial cells  just to show that this is pervasive  across all subtypes it's not just one  subtype essentially by redistributing  these reads we do a fantastic job of  collapsing  the differences that were initially just  inherent noise within the data  you can imagine again that in the  standard condition  if we have genes that are only captured  by those cells simply because  they were dropped out in the sequencing  data within the other cells that they  essentially end up being interpreted as  phenotypic signal  by removing those and redistributing  reads more evenly across lowly expressed  and capturing more unique molecules we  can see a reduction in this technical  noise and we do a better job of  interpreting the biological data  now you might remember that one of the  main goals of this was to be able to  discern transcriptional differences  between coronary and pulmonary  now what you're seeing here are the  results of batch correction following  the same exact batch correction the same  exact computational methods on the left  when you have again batch correction  fifty percent of these cells are  coronary fifty percent of these cells  all are pulmonary what i'm highlighting  in the purple dashed lines  is that massive regions where the batch  correction essentially does an  incredibly poor job of overlaying cells  of these different different types and  one and creating one integrated manifold  interpretation  now you can see that on the right the  coronary and pulmonary essentially every  single colored subgroup within that  graph  is represented in both coronary and  pulmonary incredibly evenly it's almost  as if they're mirror images of each  other except for only that one region  that i've highlighted in purple down  below  again this intuitively makes sense these  are endothelial cells from blood vessels  the only difference so they're the exact  same cells the only difference is where  those blood vessels are located  so when we perform a differential  expression analysis between the coronary  and pulmonary cells what i'm showing you  here are the results of the top eight  genes that come out of that analysis so  the first four represented in the x-axis  on the top  are genes that are essentially up  regulated within coronary pulmonary  within coronary excuse me and then the  next four are the top four genes that  are down regulated in coronary relative  to pulmonary as you can see the same  genes are showing up from the analysis  so again we're not losing any of this  biological signal  but you can see if we wanted to pinpoint  which of these subgroups is really the  differentiating factor  lending to this difference between  coronary and pulmonary cells if you  focused on the heat matrix on the left  within the standard condition it would  be incredibly hard to pinpoint any one  of these subgroups as the main causal  factor kind of driving this difference  between coronary and pulmonary  but right by removing that technical  noise and adding an additional 2 000  unique molecules per cell across 50 000  cells  you can see it becomes incredibly clear  that there's really only one sub  subgroup that is driving this difference  between coronary and pulmonary  endothelial cells  now just to confirm that our findings  are on track with previously published  data sets in bulk analysis  what i'm showing you here is a network  diagram the gene set enrichment on the  left of that one subgroup and you can  see highlighted in purple  are the genes that are playing the most  significant part in these biological  processes  that you can see on the bottom right  things like blood  vessel morphogenesis  external encapsulating structure  organization and extracellular matrix  organization  now what's interesting is that all of  these genes have been previously  identified in bulk analysis  leading to the buildup of plaque within  coronary blood vessels  so this essentially shows that  while depletion increases this  biological interpretation that it's not  something incredibly nuanced that it  makes sense based off of previous  published data sets and bulk analysis  so we did a fantastic job of removing  about 36 percent of unwanted reads and  redistributing those leading to an  increase in about 2 000 unique molecules  per cell  but are there any other reads within our  data that can be removed that are  unnecessary to interpret any sort of  biology  so what i'm showing you here is the  exact same schematic that i showed at  the beginning  the light blue representing the  non-polyadenylated ribosomal reeds the  dark purple representing the  polyadenylated ribosomal and  mitochondrial reeds and now we're adding  two extra groups that we have targeted  for depletion  one represented in kind of this  turquoise right next to the ribosomal  rna reads is what's labeled as  unaligned reads and unaligned reads  essentially are these consensus  intervals across all sample types and  tissue types  that are these genomic intervals that  essentially show up across all of these  data sets perhaps they are poly a  stretches that get captured and  amplified during the rt generation  and second  represented in the light purple next to  the dark purple rib ribometal are these  non-variable genes and you can think of  these as highly expressed housekeeping  genes whose expression does not change  across cell types  so essentially if it doesn't change  across cell types then it plays no role  in interpreting and distinguishing  different cell types within single cell  rna-seq data and is perfect for removal  so what we did to ensure that this was a  robust group of genes in regions  targeted for removal we essentially  performed in silicon depletion of 14  publicly available single cell sra  datasets  so on the far left if we only remove the  non-polydenylated ribosomal rna that  represents about 10 percent of aligned  breeds  in dark purple if we only removed  the top expressed ribosomal and  mitochondrial genes represents about 30  percent of reads remember these data  points is across all 14 sample and  tissue types  if we combine those two together we get  up to about 42 percent  now if we add on in addition to those  guide sets if we add on  the guides targeting those unaligned  genomic intervals we can get up to about  50 of reeds that could be removed and  redistributed to lowly expressed rare  and unique molecules  and lastly if we add on those  non-variable genes the genes that aren't  playing any role in biological  interpretation we can get up to about 58  60 percent of our reeds that could be  removed and redistributed  so the schematic i'm showing you here is  the experimental overview  of what we performed combining all of  these guide sets within pbmc samples so  what we're showing you is on the far  left is that we're comparing our  depleted conditions that were performed  in triplicate against the control and  the control here again is just the  standard 10x workflow and same as before  each of these control versus depleted  condition  represents  wta splits of the exact same cell types  so right off the bat if we look at the  distribution of reads in the far left on  the control  you can see in green that represents not  informative transcriptional reads in  blue represents informative  transcriptional reads and in purple  represents the rest of the reeds that  align to the genome and thus aren't used  now on the right what you can see  is a almost twofold enhancement in that  blue region or in other words a 100  percent increase in the reads that map  to the transcriptome that are  informative  now if we dive in a little bit further  and look on a cell by cell basis you can  see on the far left a sequencing  saturation curve that is as we increase  in sequencing depth per cell we're  simply recovering more and more unique  molecules per cell  at the end of the day at a sequencing  depth of about 25 000 reads per cell  depletion recovers an additional 280  genes per cell  and about additional 500 unique  molecules per cell  and this correlates to an additional  2000 genes that contribute to biological  signal that otherwise would not have  been found within the control  now before i jump into some of the umap  plots  for those of you that are familiar with  the analysis of single cell rna-seq i  want to ensure everyone that i am not  and did not perform any manual  annotation of this data set  in order to  automatically identify the cell types  within pbmc databases  we leverage a tool that uses predefined  legacy markers so this is an automatic  annotation tool that essentially  references massive repositories of  single cell pbmc data  containing over a million cells  so when we use that tool on both data  sites  you can see here some of the canonical  cell types that show in with show up  within pbmc cells those included in the  far left like pink are showing up these  natural killer cells  and on the right those same natural  killer cells are now in the bottom right  corner identified in blue so you can see  we're we're recovering these same exact  sound types  with one  notable difference  on the left  kind of highlighted and circled in this  purple dash line  you can see that  the control is unable to distinguish  between the cd4 monocytes and these two  kind of arms that are branching out from  the cd-14 monocytes  whereas in the depleted condition you  can see one of those arms actually  separate within transcriptional space  and identify and distinguish between  cd14 and cd16 monocytes  in addition it also has the ability to  characterize these rare cell types of  dendritic cells cd1c positive dendritic  cells that represent less than one  percent of the cell population in  absolute frequency  just to confirm that the results  previously mentioned were not a factor  of the computational analysis or  automatic annotation tool  what we did is we performed this  unsupervised cell clustering that uses  deep learning to automatically identify  the proper number of clusters  represented within your data  using this tool what we found is that  depletion still recovers two additional  cell types using this unsupervised  clustering approach  on the far left you can see labeled the  control sample we find about nine  clusters  and the depleted sample we find about 11  clusters  you can see on the bottom left in the  control cluster number six the depletion  because of the redistribution of genes  and the additional 2000  genes that are playing a part in  biological interpretation cluster number  six is split into two individual  clusters  and lastly this kind of arm that  connects cluster one and cluster 0 in  the control is essentially totally  separated into its own cluster and you  can see there is no longer any sort of  connection between cluster 0 and cluster  1.  in addition to give us some confidence  in assuring that these two additional  clusters are actually real  this tool also assigns a probability of  those clusters being separate clusters  the probability you can see as a colored  heat matrix on the right of this graph  going from about  0.2 being yellow all the way up to 0.8.9  represented in dark blue dark purple  so you can see those two those  additional clusters in the bottom right  that were separated as well as those in  the top right in the depleted this tool  assigns a very high confidence a high  probability assuring us that these are  actually two different clusters  so what i've showed you up to this point  is that  we can sequence the same  and recover more information by  performing depletion  but  on the other end of that coin  can we sequence less perhaps half the  number of sequencing reads but recover  the same exact information using crispr  clean depletion  so what i'm showing you here are the raw  metrics within this table  this first column represents the control  or the non-depleted so at a sequencing  depth about 27 000 reads per cell you  can see the same number of cells in that  second row  but on the bottom two rows genes per  cell we recover about a thousand three  hundred genes per cell and about close  to two thousand unique molecules per  cell  now the second column represents if we  just took half the same exact sample and  sequence to half you can see here again  representing about thirteen thousand  five hundred reads per cell  we don't recover exactly half but we  recover significantly less genes per  cell in um is per cell in those bottom  two rows  now  if we take essentially that same exact  sample of cells  perform depletion and only sequence to  half you can see we recover  the number of genes per cell and the  number of unique molecules per cell  and on the right what i'm showing you is  a distribution of those reads and genes  per cell and um i counts per cell of the  depletion at half the sequencing depth  and the control at the full sequencing  depth you can see there is no bias in  the distribution of the data and almost  a perfect overlay of the counts per cell  and genes per cell  even more surprisingly now when we  compare the umap plots of the control at  27 000 reads per cell and they depleted  at half the sequencing depth in the  depleted condition we surprisingly still  recover the ability to identify those  two additional cell types that were  unable to be identified within the  control sample  so just to recap i would like to point  out that we recover an additional  essentially 100 percent boost in  informative reads within our pbmc  samples  we recover an additional about 1.5 fold  increase in genes per cell and unique  molecules recovered per cell  and this enables us to recover an  additional two  cell types within our pbmc sample data  that could not have been identified  otherwise  so all in all depletion increases the  sensitivity of single cell  experiments and allows one to recover  the same information  at half the number of sequencing reads  so in conclusion depletion recovers more  information per cell  we  increase the signal to noise by  performing depletion  and by redistributing those reads  depletion statistically enhances the  biological interpretation  i would like to thank the entire team  here at jumpcode genomics  as well as the pandey lab at scripps  research as well as the scripps genomics  score that helped with the sequencing  this was a team effort and could not  have been done alone and please let me  know if you have any questions  thank you john for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started  our first question is does the crispr  clean reaction use all the c dna from my  10x prep  yes that's a great question so the  answer is no it doesn't so the  traditional 10x workflow  is after you generate the  oil droplets with your cells  go through and generate the cdna or the  wta is what they call it the whole  transcriptome amplification  at that point you come to a nice  stopping point  and  at that point what you can do is you can  quantify your cdna and you actually  split your cdna into four equivalent  groups even according to the protocol  so what you do here is in depletion is  you just take one of those splits  and perform the depletion on one of  those splits so if you want to compare  it  to a control like you saw at this  presentation with our two experiments  then you essentially only use two out of  the four splits one would be your  control your standard tenants processing  and then the other one would be for your  depletion go through the protocol  which also raises another excellent  point then i just want to reiterate is  that all the experiments that you had  seen because we're splitting it at the  cdna the controls are depleted represent  the same exact cells so the comparisons  here are incredibly robust as if you're  comparing if you had 10 000 cells it's  almost as if you're comparing every cell  against itself  okay  thank you  next question  does crispr clean depletion introduce  bias to my gene expression readout  no absolutely not so this is one of the  first things that we looked for  and so if you take  every gene in a control sample in every  gene  in a depleted sample and if you look at  simply the correlation or the r squared  value of all 15 or 17 000 genes that you  have  it would be incredibly close to one it's  all the samples you've seen are 0.98  0.99 so essentially round up to 1. so  the key here is that we're not changing  the distribution of the genes or  changing the relative expression we're  simply providing kind of more value for  what you would get where you take the  reeds that are typically  totally ignored and removed  computationally we're taking those  reasons redistributing them to lowly  express genes and unique molecules that  otherwise wouldn't be captured so we're  just increasing the sensitivity and  specificity without  distrib  biasing the gene expression  okay thank you  next question  where can i find detailed information on  this kit  yeah absolutely so if you go to jumpco  genomics.com  and you can browse through our website  there you will see some  very much detailed information about  some of the specifics  on this kit and if you remember at the  beginning of this as this presentation  look for  crispr clean  sc boost  and you'll be able to find more  information about the experiments that  we reviewed today some of the detailed  metrics as well as some of the  actual raw data if you want to peruse  through the data yourself  and kind of check out that we see this  added  boosts and unique molecules and genes  and you can also download the  information on our website as well  great  well it looks like we have time for one  more question is this kit compatible  with the five prime chromium assay what  about vision spatial  yeah so that's a great question  um so one of the reasons if you remember  about the middle of this presentation  that we did a deep dive into accessing  publicly available data as we wanted to  make a product that was not only  universal  really across different biological  samples and different replicates and as  well as different tissue types but we  wanted to make sure that the reads that  we are seeing were also found in other  kits and other assays  and so we have now done some preliminary  experiments within a five prime chromium  assay and it worked fantastic  as well as some preliminary experience  experiments within the vision spatial  and we see very similar effects where  we're able to remove these  unwanted unusable reads and redistribute  them to lowly express read so yes it  seems like this is pervasive across a  lot of platforms within 1